00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
the sudden spread of monkey pox has  puzzled scientists so what do we know  well this is the virus first identifying  laboratory monkeys in 1958 then in  humans in 1970 there are two strains  endemic to parts of africa the central  african subtype can cause severe  symptoms including large lesions on the  skin with a fatality rate of around 10  percent the west african version causing  the current outbreak results in milder  symptoms including a rash that blisters  and scabs over a bit like chicken pox it  can be worse than children infected  people can also have flu-like symptoms  of fever muscle aches and pains chills  and exhaustion  in africa the virus is primarily spread  by wild animals including rodents it  doesn't transmit easily between people  but the virus can spread by direct skin  to skin contact towels bedding and the  clothing of people with active infection  as well as droplets from coughs and  sneezes but isn't thought to be airborne  like kovid  before the current outbreak the seven uk  cases since 2018 were all in travelers  returning from west africa or people in  their household now that's what makes  this outbreak so unusual with  communities spread in multiple countries  well away from endemic regions  so what could be driving the sudden  surge well first the virus could have  changed to become more transmissible  most scientists think this is unlikely  it is a dna virus with two strands of  genetic material that are in effect an  autocorrect feature that stops the rapid  mutation rate seen in single stranded  rna viruses like kovid  the second possibility is that it is now  by chance in people who have more social  contacts according to the uk health  security agency most but not all cases  are gay bisexual or men who have sex  with men it says the virus may be  spreading through sexual networks not a  sexually transmitted disease in the  normal sense but through close and  sometimes intimate physical contact  people with the virus and their direct  or household contacts are being asked to  self-isolate for three weeks  infected people generally recover  without any treatment and the context  may be offered a smallpox vaccine which  is also effective at stopping symptoms  of monkey pox  the risk of picking up the virus is low  we're not in a covet situation but this  is another animal virus spreading  between people for reasons scientists  can't yet explain and they're keen to  shut it down
 

